{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Portfolio Return Prediction and Construction with Ridge Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "A rolling-window machine-learning framework for predicting stock returns from financial characteristics and evaluating prediction-based portfolio signals.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Academic context.** This notebook is a portfolio adaptation of work originally developed during graduate coursework in *Applied Machine Learning in Finance* at the University of Melbourne. It has been reorganised and revised for public presentation. Course materials and restricted data are not included.\n",
    "\n",
    "The analysis is educational and does not constitute investment advice."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-00"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Project Overview\n",
    "\n",
    "The project studies how Ridge regularisation affects out-of-sample return forecasts and the risk-adjusted performance of prediction-weighted portfolios. It uses a three-year rolling training window, predicts the following 12 months, and retrains annually across 11 values of $alpha$ from $10^{-5}$ to $10^5$.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main empirical distinction is between:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **realised performance:** predicted-return weights multiplied by subsequent realised returns; and\n",
    "- **model-implied expected performance:** the same weights multiplied by predicted returns.\n",
    "\n",
    "This distinction allows the model's ex-ante view to be compared with ex-post outcomes."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-01"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\", context=\"notebook\")\n",
    "pd.set_option(\"display.float_format\", lambda x: f\"{x:,.3f}\")\n",
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-02"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data & Features\n",
    "\n",
    "The original course dataset contains a panel of monthly firm-level financial characteristics and a forward return target covering approximately 1970–2000. It is not redistributed because access and reuse rights belong to the original course/data provider.\n",
    "\n",
    "To run the notebook with an authorised copy, place it at `data/raw/factor_and_ret_oos.csv`. The file must contain `id`, `date`, `ret_future`, and the characteristic columns used below. No raw observations are embedded in this notebook."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-03"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "DATA_PATH = Path(\"data/raw/factor_and_ret_oos.csv\")\n",
    "\n",
    "if not DATA_PATH.exists():\n",
    "    raise FileNotFoundError(\n",
    "        \"Restricted source data are not included. Place an authorised copy at \"\n",
    "        f\"{DATA_PATH.resolve()} and rerun the notebook.\"\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(DATA_PATH, index_col=[\"id\", \"date\"], parse_dates=[\"date\"])\n",
    "df = df.drop(columns=[\"Unnamed: 0\"], errors=\"ignore\").sort_index()\n",
    "\n",
    "future_return = df[[\"ret_future\"]].copy()\n",
    "raw_factors = df.drop(columns=\"ret_future\").copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Observations: {len(df):,}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Firm-level characteristics: {raw_factors.shape[1]:,}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Date range: {df.index.get_level_values('date').min():%Y-%m-%d} to \"\n",
    "      f\"{df.index.get_level_values('date').max():%Y-%m-%d}\")"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-04"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Rolling Factor Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "Book leverage (`at_be`), six-month momentum (`ret_6_0`) and market beta (`beta_60m`) are examined using three-year windows advanced in six-month steps. Missing observations are set to zero for this diagnostic, matching the original analytical convention.\n",
    "\n",
    "Time variation matters even when pairwise correlations are weak: changing dependence among characteristics can alter diversification and portfolio risk. This motivates rolling estimation rather than assuming one static relationship over the full sample."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-05"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "factor_labels = {\n",
    "    \"at_be\": \"Book leverage vs momentum\",\n",
    "    \"ret_6_0\": \"Momentum vs market beta\",\n",
    "    \"beta_60m\": \"Book leverage vs market beta\",\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "dates = df.index.get_level_values(\"date\")\n",
    "window = pd.DateOffset(months=36)\n",
    "step = pd.DateOffset(months=6)\n",
    "records = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for window_end in pd.date_range(dates.min() + window, dates.max(), freq=step):\n",
    "    in_window = (dates >= window_end - window) & (dates < window_end)\n",
    "    corr = df.loc[in_window, [\"at_be\", \"ret_6_0\", \"beta_60m\"]].fillna(0).corr()\n",
    "    records.append({\n",
    "        \"date\": window_end,\n",
    "        \"Book leverage vs momentum\": corr.loc[\"at_be\", \"ret_6_0\"],\n",
    "        \"Momentum vs market beta\": corr.loc[\"ret_6_0\", \"beta_60m\"],\n",
    "        \"Book leverage vs market beta\": corr.loc[\"at_be\", \"beta_60m\"],\n",
    "    })\n",
    "\n",
    "rolling_correlations = pd.DataFrame(records).set_index(\"date\")\n",
    "rolling_correlations.head()"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-06"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ax = rolling_correlations.plot(figsize=(12, 6), linewidth=1.8)\n",
    "ax.axhline(0, color=\"black\", linestyle=\"--\", linewidth=0.8)\n",
    "ax.set(title=\"Rolling Three-Year Factor Correlations\", xlabel=\"Window end\", ylabel=\"Correlation\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-07"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data Preprocessing\n",
    "\n",
    "Characteristics with at least 25% missing observations are removed. Retained features are standardised across the available sample and remaining missing values are replaced with zero, corresponding to the post-standardisation mean.\n",
    "\n",
    "This preserves the preprocessing convention used in the original analysis. In a production forecasting system, scaling parameters should instead be estimated inside each training window to eliminate any possible look-ahead in preprocessing."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-08"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "missing_share = raw_factors.isna().mean()\n",
    "features_kept = missing_share[missing_share < 0.25].index\n",
    "\n",
    "factors = raw_factors.loc[:, features_kept].copy()\n",
    "feature_std = factors.std().replace(0, np.nan)\n",
    "factors = ((factors - factors.mean()) / feature_std).fillna(0.0)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Retained {len(features_kept)} of {raw_factors.shape[1]} characteristics.\")"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-09"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Ridge Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "Ridge regression shrinks coefficients through an $L_2$ penalty. The regularisation strength $\u0007lpha$ controls the trade-off between fitting cross-sectional return signals and stabilising predictions."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-10"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def predict_ridge(X_train, y_train, X_test, alpha):\n",
    "    # Fit Ridge regression and return one-dimensional predictions.\n",
    "    model = Ridge(alpha=alpha, solver=\"lsqr\")\n",
    "    model.fit(X_train, np.asarray(y_train).ravel())\n",
    "    return model.predict(X_test)"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-11"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Rolling Out-of-Sample Prediction\n",
    "\n",
    "Each model is estimated using the previous 36 months and then used to forecast the following 12 months. Annual retraining respects the time ordering of the data. Predictions are stored alongside the realised forward return for each firm-month."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-12"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "log_alphas = np.arange(-5.0, 6.0)\n",
    "monthly_dates = np.sort(factors.index.get_level_values(\"date\").unique())\n",
    "prediction_blocks = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for start_idx in range(36, len(monthly_dates), 12):\n",
    "    train_dates = monthly_dates[start_idx - 36:start_idx]\n",
    "    test_dates = monthly_dates[start_idx:min(start_idx + 12, len(monthly_dates))]\n",
    "\n",
    "    is_train = factors.index.get_level_values(\"date\").isin(train_dates)\n",
    "    is_test = factors.index.get_level_values(\"date\").isin(test_dates)\n",
    "    if not is_test.any():\n",
    "        continue\n",
    "\n",
    "    X_train, y_train = factors.loc[is_train], future_return.loc[is_train, \"ret_future\"]\n",
    "    X_test = factors.loc[is_test]\n",
    "    block = future_return.loc[is_test].copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "    for log_alpha in log_alphas:\n",
    "        column = f\"logalpha_{log_alpha:.1f}\"\n",
    "        block[column] = predict_ridge(X_train, y_train, X_test, 10 ** log_alpha)\n",
    "\n",
    "    prediction_blocks.append(block)\n",
    "\n",
    "df_pred = pd.concat(prediction_blocks).sort_index()\n",
    "df_pred.head()"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-13"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Portfolio Construction\n",
    "\n",
    "For each $\u0007lpha$, the predicted return is used directly as the portfolio weight:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$w_{i,t}(\u0007lpha)=\\hat r_{i,t}(\u0007lpha).$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Monthly realised portfolio return is therefore $\\sum_i w_{i,t}r_{i,t+1}$. These are signal portfolios rather than fully invested long-only portfolios; weights are not normalised to sum to one."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-14"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "prediction_columns = [c for c in df_pred if c.startswith(\"logalpha_\")]\n",
    "\n",
    "realised_returns = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    column: (df_pred[column] * df_pred[\"ret_future\"]).groupby(\"date\").sum()\n",
    "    for column in prediction_columns\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "def annualised_sharpe(monthly_returns):\n",
    "    volatility = monthly_returns.std()\n",
    "    return np.sqrt(12) * monthly_returns.mean() / volatility if volatility != 0 else np.nan\n",
    "\n",
    "realised_sharpe = pd.Series(\n",
    "    {column: annualised_sharpe(realised_returns[column]) for column in prediction_columns},\n",
    "    name=\"realised_sharpe\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "realised_summary = realised_sharpe.rename_axis(\"prediction\").reset_index()\n",
    "realised_summary[\"log_alpha\"] = realised_summary[\"prediction\"].str.replace(\"logalpha_\", \"\", regex=False).astype(float)\n",
    "realised_summary[\"alpha\"] = 10 ** realised_summary[\"log_alpha\"]\n",
    "realised_summary[[\"alpha\", \"realised_sharpe\"]].sort_values(\"alpha\")"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-15"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Hyperparameter Evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "The archived course run reported the strongest realised annualised Sharpe ratio at $\u0007lpha=1{,}000$ (Sharpe $=0.309$). The table below is retained as a documented result from that run; rerunning the preceding cells recreates it from an authorised copy of the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Alpha | Realised Sharpe |\n",
    "|---:|---:|\n",
    "| 0.00001 | 0.059 |\n",
    "| 0.00010 | 0.059 |\n",
    "| 0.00100 | 0.059 |\n",
    "| 0.01000 | 0.059 |\n",
    "| 0.10000 | 0.057 |\n",
    "| 1 | 0.073 |\n",
    "| 10 | 0.103 |\n",
    "| 100 | 0.126 |\n",
    "| **1,000** | **0.309** |\n",
    "| 10,000 | 0.227 |\n",
    "| 100,000 | 0.108 |"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-16"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "best_realised = realised_summary.loc[realised_summary[\"realised_sharpe\"].idxmax()]\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 5))\n",
    "ax.plot(realised_summary[\"log_alpha\"], realised_summary[\"realised_sharpe\"], marker=\"o\")\n",
    "ax.axvline(best_realised[\"log_alpha\"], color=\"tab:red\", linestyle=\"--\", alpha=0.7)\n",
    "ax.set(title=\"Realised Sharpe Ratio Across Ridge Penalties\",\n",
    "       xlabel=r\"$\\log_{10}(\u0007lpha)$\", ylabel=\"Annualised Sharpe ratio\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Best realised alpha: {best_realised['alpha']:g}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Best realised Sharpe ratio: {best_realised['realised_sharpe']:.3f}\")"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-17"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Expected vs Realised Performance\n",
    "\n",
    "Model-implied expected portfolio return uses the prediction both as the weight and as the expected asset return:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$E_t[r_{p,t+1}]=\\sum_i w_{i,t}\\hat r_{i,t}=\\sum_i \\hat r_{i,t}^2.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The archived run selected $\u0007lpha=100{,}000$ using expected Sharpe (2.754), while the realised-performance selection was $\u0007lpha=1{,}000$. This gap is itself informative, but comparing those two settings directly would compare different portfolios.\n",
    "\n",
    "The corrected comparison below first selects the expected-Sharpe winner, then fixes that single $\u0007lpha$ and its weights for both series. Expected return is prediction × prediction; realised return is the **same prediction-derived weight** × realised future return."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-18"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "expected_returns = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    column: (df_pred[column] * df_pred[column]).groupby(\"date\").sum()\n",
    "    for column in prediction_columns\n",
    "})\n",
    "expected_sharpe = pd.Series(\n",
    "    {column: annualised_sharpe(expected_returns[column]) for column in prediction_columns},\n",
    "    name=\"expected_sharpe\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "comparison_alpha_column = expected_sharpe.idxmax()\n",
    "comparison_log_alpha = float(comparison_alpha_column.replace(\"logalpha_\", \"\"))\n",
    "comparison_alpha = 10 ** comparison_log_alpha\n",
    "\n",
    "expected_same_portfolio = expected_returns[comparison_alpha_column]\n",
    "realised_same_portfolio = realised_returns[comparison_alpha_column]\n",
    "\n",
    "comparison = pd.concat(\n",
    "    [expected_same_portfolio.rename(\"Expected\"), realised_same_portfolio.rename(\"Realised\")],\n",
    "    axis=1,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax = comparison.plot(figsize=(12, 6), linewidth=1.3, alpha=0.85)\n",
    "ax.axhline(0, color=\"black\", linewidth=0.8)\n",
    "ax.set(title=f\"Expected vs Realised Returns for the Same Portfolio (alpha={comparison_alpha:g})\",\n",
    "       xlabel=\"Date\", ylabel=\"Monthly portfolio return\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    \"series\": [\"Expected\", \"Realised\"],\n",
    "    \"alpha\": [comparison_alpha, comparison_alpha],\n",
    "    \"annualised_sharpe\": [annualised_sharpe(expected_same_portfolio),\n",
    "                           annualised_sharpe(realised_same_portfolio)],\n",
    "})"
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-19"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Key Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Pairwise factor correlations were generally weak but varied over time, supporting a rolling framework for changing dependence and portfolio risk.\n",
    "2. Regularisation materially affected portfolio behaviour. Moderate shrinkage produced the best realised risk-adjusted result in the archived run ($\u0007lpha=1{,}000$, Sharpe 0.309), while very strong shrinkage produced the highest model-implied expected Sharpe ($\u0007lpha=100{,}000$, Sharpe 2.754).\n",
    "3. The ex-ante winner did not match the ex-post winner. Model-implied performance should therefore not be interpreted as realised investable performance.\n",
    "4. The expected-versus-realised comparison must hold $\u0007lpha$ and portfolio weights fixed. The corrected analysis does so, making the two return series directly comparable.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "- The dataset is restricted and is not redistributed, so full reproduction requires authorised access.\n",
    "- The portfolios use raw prediction signals as weights and do not incorporate transaction costs, turnover limits, leverage constraints or a risk-free rate.\n",
    "- Global feature standardisation follows the original analytical convention; production research should estimate transformations within each training window.\n",
    "- Results are historical and sample-specific."
   ],
   "id": "portfolio-20"
  }
 ],
